{ Welcome to the Stafford gambit, a risky trap weapon for quick games. You give a pawn on move three, and every piece you own aims at his king. With perfect defence he is better, but his natural moves walk into traps. }
1. e4 { He opens with his king pawn; the queen pawn to d4 would call for another study. Meet it head on: push your own king pawn two squares. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 { Your pawn takes its share of the middle and opens roads for your queen and your dark bishop. If his bishop comes to c4 next, that is another opening. [%cal Gd8h4,Gf8c5] } (1... c5 { The Sicilian: sharp and very good, but another opening. } 2. Nf3 d6 { A study of its own. Today your king pawn meets his head on. [%csl Bc5] }) (1... e6 { The French: solid, but that pawn shuts your light bishop in. } 2. d4 d5 { A patient, blocked game, and another study. Today we want open lines. [%csl Be6] }) (1... c6 { The Caro-Kann: solid and slow. } 2. d4 d5 { A calm game, and another study. Today we want the sharp, open kind. [%csl Bc6] }) (1... d5 { The Scandinavian: you hit his pawn at once. } 2. exd5 Nf6 { Slightly better for him: his pawn took yours, and it is another opening. Today your king pawn goes two squares. [%csl Bd5] }) (1... d6 { The Pirc family: flexible, but it lets him take the whole middle. } 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 { About level: another opening, where he builds a big centre first. [%csl Bd6] }) 2. Nf3 { His knight hits your pawn on e5. Do not guard it: bring your king knight out to f6 and hit his pawn instead. [%cal Yg8f6,Rf3e5] } 2... Nf6 { Your knight hits his pawn in the middle, just as his knight hits yours. If he guards with knight c3 or bishop c4 instead, that is another opening. [%cal Gf6e4] } (2... Nc6 { The most played move, and just as good: the knight guards your pawn. But it leads to other openings. } 3. Bb5 a6 { That is the Ruy Lopez, a study of its own. Today your knight hits his pawn instead. [%csl Bc6] }) (2... d6 { Just as good by the numbers: the pawn guards your king pawn. But it shuts your dark bishop in. } 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 { About level: that is the Philidor, solid and slow. Today we want every piece out fast. [%csl Bf8] }) 3. Nxe5 { He grabs your pawn. A pawn to d4 would be another opening; this one is ours. Leave his knight alone and offer your queen knight on c6: a pawn for fast pieces aimed at his king. [%cal Yb8c6] } 3... Nc6 { That is the Stafford gambit: you give a pawn so that your pieces come out first and all aim at his king. Your knight hits his knight on e5. [%cal Gc6e5] } (3... d6 { The sound move, and objectively better: the pawn kicks his knight and wins the pawn back. That is the Petrov, but it sets no traps. } 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 d5 { About level, quiet and correct. Today you trade that safety for traps. [%csl Bd6] }) (3... Nxe4 { Also sound: take his pawn back at once. } 4. Qe2 Qe7 5. Qxe4 d6 6. d4 dxe5 7. dxe5 { About level: a knight each came off, and your queen already hits his pawn on e5. Quiet, with no traps. [%csl Be5] }) (3... Qe7 { Objectively better than the gambit too: the queen wins the pawn back. But she stands in front of your own bishop. } 4. d4 d6 5. Nf3 Qxe4+ 6. Be2 { Slightly better for him: you won the pawn back, but his pieces come out faster. [%csl Re7] }) (3... Bc5 { Another gambit, just as risky, but here his pawns chase the bishop. } 4. d4 Bb6 5. Nc3 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and his pawns own the middle. [%csl Rb6] }) (3... d5 { Objectively a little better than the gambit: a strike in the middle. But his queen comes to e2. } 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. Qe2 { Better for him: he keeps the pawn, and his queen lines up with your king on the e column. [%csl Re8] }) (3... Be7 { Objectively a little better than the gambit: calm development. But you are simply a pawn down. } 4. Nc3 O-O 5. d4 { Slightly better for him: a quiet game, and nothing to show for the pawn. [%csl Be7] }) 4. Nxc6 { He takes your knight. Had his knight gone back to f3, your knight would simply take e4. Take back with the d pawn: it opens the d column for your queen. [%cal Yd7c6] } 4... dxc6 { Your d pawn takes back. The d column is open for your queen, and your light bishop is free: every piece has a road to his king. [%cal Gd8d1,Gc8g4] } (4... bxc6 { Just as good by the numbers, but your d pawn stays home and shuts your light bishop in. } 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. e5 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and his pawn chases your knight. [%csl Rc8] }) (4... Nxe4 { Grabbing the pawn back forgets his knight on c6: it hits your queen. } 5. Nxd8 Kxd8 { Lost for you: your king took his knight, but you are a queen down. [%csl Rd8] }) 5. d3 { He guards his king pawn with the d pawn. Bring your dark bishop to c5: it stares at f2, the square only his king guards. [%cal Yf8c5,Gc5f2] } 5... Bc5 { Your bishop points at f2. Your queen and knight will join it there, and that is where his traps begin. [%csl Rf2] } (5... Be6 { Just as good: the bishop develops. But on e6 it does not touch his king. } 6. Be2 Qe7 7. Be3 { Better for him, and quiet: he keeps the pawn, and nothing of yours hits his king. [%csl Be6] }) (5... Qe7 { Just as good: the queen eyes the e column. But the bishop comes first in this gambit. } 6. Be2 Be6 7. Be3 { Better for him, and quiet: he keeps the pawn, and your bishop never reached c5. [%csl Be6] }) 6. Be2 { He tucks his bishop on e2, ready to castle. Push your h pawn two squares: it guards g4 for your knight. [%cal Yh7h5,Gh5g4] } 6... h5 { Your h pawn guards g4. If his bishop ever takes a knight there, the pawn takes back and your rook gets the h column. Pawn h3 from him? Then your queen comes out instead. [%cal Gh5g4,Gh8h1] } (6... Ng4 { Objectively a touch better: the knight jumps at once. But his bishop takes it, and the queens come off. } 7. Bxg4 Qh4 8. Qf3 Qxg4 9. Qxg4 Bxg4 { Better for him: a quiet ending, and you are simply a pawn down with no traps left. [%csl Rg4] }) (6... O-O { Castling is fine, but it sets nothing up: your h pawn stays home. } 7. O-O Ne8 8. Nd2 { Better for him: he castled too, and his extra pawn is safe. [%csl Bh7] }) (6... Be6 { Just as good: develop first. } 7. c3 Qe7 8. O-O { Better for him: a normal game, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%csl Be6] }) (6... Qd4 { Just as good by the numbers: the queen jumps out early. But he castles, and his c pawn chases her. } 7. O-O Be6 8. c3 Qd7 { Much better for him: your queen had to walk back to d7. [%csl Rd7] }) (6... Qe7 { Just as good, even a little better: the queen develops, and your king can castle long. } 7. c3 Bb6 8. O-O { Better for him: a calm game, and his extra pawn stays. [%csl Be7] }) (6... h6 { Just as good: the pawn keeps his bishop off g5. } 7. c3 a5 8. O-O { Better for him: a calm game, where his extra pawn stays safe. [%csl Bh6] }) (6... Bb6 { Just as good: the bishop steps back before his pawns can chase it. } 7. c3 Qe7 8. O-O { Better for him: your bishop is safe, but nothing of yours hits his king yet. [%csl Bb6] }) 7. c3 { He plays the calm c pawn move, ready to push to d4. That is his best, and there is no trick against it: bring your light bishop out to e6. [%cal Yc8e6] } 7... Be6 { Your bishop is out, and your king can castle long soon. If his bishop pins your knight from g5, the queen steps to d7. [%cal Ge8c8] } (7... Ng4 { Just as good by the numbers, and the most played. But pawn to d4 hits your bishop and takes the knight's squares. } 8. d4 Bd6 9. Nd2 { Better for him: your knight has nothing to hit, and his pawns own the middle. [%csl Rg4] }) (7... Bb6 { Just as good: the bishop steps back before his pawn arrives. } 8. a4 a5 { Better for him, the same kind of game: a pawn down, pieces out. [%csl Bb6] }) (7... Bg4 { Just as good by the numbers: the bishop offers a trade. } 8. f3 Be6 9. d4 { Better for him: his pawn chased your bishop back, and he took the middle. [%csl Rf3] }) (7... Bd6 { Just as good: the bishop steps back before his pawn arrives, and heads for f4. } 8. Nd2 Bf4 9. h3 { Better for him: the bishops may come off, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Bd6] }) (7... Qe7 { Just as good: the queen develops first, and the bishop follows. } 8. Nd2 Be6 9. Nf3 { Better for him: the same kind of game, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%csl Be7] }) (7... Bxf2+ { A bishop for one pawn: the check goes nowhere. } 8. Kxf2 Qd6 9. Nd2 { Much better for him: you are a piece down, and his king is safe. [%csl Rf2] }) 8. d4 { His pawn hits your bishop. Step back to b6, where it keeps its diagonal. Careful: on d6 his e pawn would fork bishop and knight. [%cal Yc5b6] } 8... Bb6 { Your bishop is safe on b6, and it presses on his pawn on d4. [%cal Gb6d4] } (8... Be7 { Just as good, even a little better: e7 is safe too. } 9. Nd2 h4 { Better for him, as in the main line: a pawn up and a big centre. [%csl Be7] }) (8... Bf8 { Just as good: the bishop goes home, where no pawn can reach it. } 9. Qc2 h4 { Better for him: your bishop is safe but passive, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Bf8] }) (8... Bd6 { His pawn forks: it hits your bishop and your knight at once. } 9. e5 Bd5 10. exd6 Bxg2 11. Rg1 { Much better for him: his pawn took your bishop, and one pawn back does not pay for it. [%csl Rd6] }) (8... Nxe4 { The knight grabs a pawn, but his pawn takes your bishop. } 9. dxc5 Qxd1+ 10. Bxd1 Nxc5 { Much better for him: you have two pawns for your bishop. [%csl Rc5] }) (8... Bxd4 { The bishop takes a pawn, and his pawn takes it back. } 9. cxd4 Nxe4 10. Nc3 { Much better for him: two pawns do not pay for a bishop. [%csl Rd4] }) 9. Bg5 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen. Break the pin: queen to d7, then castle long. Had his pawn kicked the knight instead, it would simply step aside. [%cal Yd8d7,Rg5f6] } 9... Qd7 { The queen steps off the pin and makes room to castle long. If his pawn kicks the knight now, it steps back to h7. [%cal Ge8c8] } (9... Qe7 { Just as good by the numbers: the queen clears d8 for castling long, but your knight stays pinned to her. } 10. a4 a5 11. Nd2 { Much better for him, the same game: your queen usually goes on to d7 anyway. [%csl Be7] }) (9... Nxe4 { The pin is real: his bishop takes your queen. } 10. Bxd8 Rxd8 { A queen for a bishop and a pawn: lost for you. [%csl Rd8] }) (9... Qd6 { The queen stands in front of his pawns, and they chase her. } 10. e5 Qd5 11. Bf3 Qb5 { Much better for him: your queen was kicked twice, and his pawn still hits your knight. [%csl Rf6] }) 10. Nd2 { He brings his knight out. Had his bishop taken on f6, the g pawn would take back. Now castle long: your king goes to the queen side. [%cal Ye8c8] } 10... O-O-O { That is the Stafford gambit against his best play: he is a pawn up and much better. But who plays perfectly? Your king is safe on the queen side, your pieces are out, and your h pawn is ready to run. [%csl Gc8][%cal Gh5h3] } (10... Ng4 { The knight jumps again, and his h pawn sends it home. } 11. h3 f6 12. Bf4 Nh6 13. Bxh5+ Nf7 { Much better for him: your knight was chased back, and his bishop took your h pawn. [%csl Rh5] }) (10... Nh7 { Just as good: the knight steps away from his bishop before you castle long. } 11. Be3 O-O-O { Much better for him, the same position: your king is castled a move later. [%csl Bh7] }) (10... a6 { Just as good: a quiet pawn move, and your king castles next. } 11. Qc2 O-O-O { Much better for him, the same kind of position: a pawn down, every piece out. [%csl Ba6] }) 0-1


