{ Your bishop stares at f2, and your h pawn guards g4 for your knight. His king is still in the middle. [%cal Gc5f2,Gh5g4] }
7. O-O { He castles, straight into the corner your pieces point at. That is his mistake: the calm c pawn move was best. Jump in with the knight to g4: with the bishop it hits f2 twice. [%cal Yf6g4,Gc5f2] } 7... Ng4 { Knight and bishop both hit f2. If his bishop takes on g4, your h pawn takes back and your rook gets the h column. [%cal Gg4f2,Gh8h1] } (7... h4 { Pushing the pawn on is too slow: his pawn stops it. } 8. h3 g5 9. e5 { Better for him: his pawn kicked your knight, and your attack never started. [%csl Rf6] }) (7... Qd6 { The queen aims at his king first, but his knight comes out and covers. } 8. Nd2 Bg4 9. c3 { Better for him: he is castled and a pawn up, and nothing of yours hits his king. [%csl Rg1] }) (7... Bg4 { The bishop comes out, but it trades itself off, and his king is safe. } 8. c3 Qd7 9. d4 { Better for him: your bishop will be traded, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%csl Rg4] }) (7... Be6 { A normal developing move, but his king just castled into danger, and now it gets time. } 8. Nd2 Bb6 9. Nf3 { Better for him: his knight guards the king side, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rf3] }) 8. h3 { His h pawn kicks your knight, as most players do; his bishop taking it was a little better. Leave the knight there, and bring your queen to d6. Pawn c3 would get the same queen move. [%cal Yd8d6,Gd6h2] } 8... Qd6 { That is bait: a piece you leave where he can take it, because taking it costs him more. A pawn takes, and the h column opens; a pawn to g3, and your queen takes that too. [%cal Gd6h2,Gh8h1] } (8... Qh4 { The queen to h4 looks like the attack, and most players go there. But his d pawn hits your bishop. } 9. d4 Bb6 10. c3 { Much better for him: his pawns chased your bishop off the road to f2, and your knight on g4 still has to get out. [%csl Rg4] }) (8... Ne5 { The knight runs home, and the attack is over before it began. } 9. Qd2 Be6 10. c3 { Better for him: he is castled and a pawn up, and your pieces point at nothing. [%csl Re5] }) (8... Nxf2 { A knight for a pawn, and it only opens lines for his rook. } 9. Rxf2 Bxf2+ 10. Kxf2 Qh4+ 11. Kg1 { Much better for him: you gave two pieces for a rook and a pawn, and his king is safe. [%csl Rg1] }) 9. e5 { He finds the one move that holds: his pawn blocks your queen's road to h2 and hits her. Take the pawn with your knight, not your queen. [%cal Yg4e5] } 9... Nxe5 { The knight takes the pawn back: material is level. If his rook pins it from e1, your bishop comes to f5 and your king castles long. [%csl Gd6] } (9... Qxe5 { The queen takes, and most players do. But she stands on the e column with your king behind her. } 10. Bxg4 hxg4 11. Re1 Qxe1+ 12. Qxe1+ Be6 { Much better for him: his rook came to e1, and your queen had to give herself up for it. [%csl Re1] }) (9... Qg6 { The queen steps aside, but his d pawn pushes on and your knight has to run. } 10. d4 Be7 11. Bd3 { Better for him: he gained time, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rd3] }) (9... Qd4 { The queen hits his b pawn, but his c pawn chases her off. } 10. c3 Qd7 11. d4 { Much better for him: your queen went back to d7, and his pawns took the middle. [%csl Rd7] }) 10. Bf4 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen. Unpin with a check: knight to f3. Had his bishop grabbed your h pawn instead, you would kick it with the g pawn, and never take it with the rook. [%cal Ye5f3] } 10... Nf3+ { Check! The knight jumps out of the pin, and his bishop has to take it. [%cal Gf3g1] } (10... Be6 { The bishop develops, but the pin is still there. } 11. Bg3 O-O-O 12. Nd2 { About level: your knight is still pinned, and his pieces come out. The check on f3 was cleaner. [%csl Re5] }) (10... Qf6 { The queen steps off the pin, but his queen comes out with a hit. } 11. Qd2 Ng4 12. c3 { Slightly better for him: your knight went back to g4, and his pieces are active. [%csl Rg4] }) (10... g5 { The pawn hits his bishop, but it opens your king side. } 11. Bg3 h4 12. Bh2 { Slightly better for him: your pawns came forward and your king has fewer shelters. [%csl Rg5] }) 11. Bxf3 { His bishop takes the knight. Now your queen takes his bishop on f4. [%cal Yd6f4] } 11... Qxf4 { Level: the gambit pawn came back, you have both bishops against his one, and your queen stands in the middle of his side. That was the bait at work. [%csl Gc5,Gc8] } 0-1


