{ The Stafford set-up is ready: your bishop looks at f2, and your h pawn guards g4. His king has not castled. [%cal Gc5f2,Gh5g4] }
7. Bg5 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen, and that is his mistake: the calm c pawn move was best. Must you break the pin, or is there something stronger? [%cal Rg5f6] } 7... Qd4 { Right: the queen leaves the pin, and with your bishop she hits f2, which only his king guards. His b pawn hangs as well. [%cal Gd4f2,Gc5f2] } (7... Ng4 { The knight jumps at f2, but the pin was real: his bishop takes your queen. } 8. Bxd8 Bxf2+ 9. Kf1 Ne3+ 10. Kxf2 Nxd1+ 11. Rxd1 Kxd8 { Lost for you: you won his queen back, but you are a knight down. [%csl Rd8] }) (7... Qd6 { The queen steps off the pin, but from d6 she hits nothing. } 8. Bh4 Be6 9. c3 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and your pieces have no target. [%csl Rd6] }) (7... Be7 { Breaking the pin with the bishop is safe, but slow. } 8. Nd2 Be6 9. Nf3 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and he develops in peace. [%csl Re7] }) (7... Bxf2+ { A bishop for a pawn, and the check leads nowhere. } 8. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 9. dxe4 Qxg5 10. Qd4 { Much better for him: you won his bishop back, but you gave a bishop and a knight for it. [%csl Rf2] }) (7... Be6 { The bishop develops, but the pin stays, and f2 is left alone. } 8. c3 Be7 9. h3 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and your pieces stand tied to the pin. [%csl Rf6] }) (7... Qd7 { The queen breaks the pin, but from d7 she hits nothing. } 8. c3 Be7 9. Nd2 { Better for him: a calm game, and you are simply a pawn down. [%csl Rd7] }) (7... Bg4 { The bishop hits his bishop, but he just develops, and the pin stays. } 8. Nc3 Qd6 9. Qd2 { Better for him: his pieces come out, and your gambit pawn is gone. [%csl Rg4] }) (7... Qe7 { The queen steps to e7, but your knight stays pinned to her, and she has nothing to hit. } 8. c3 Qd6 9. Bc1 { Better for him: your queen wandered, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Re7] }) (7... O-O { Castling is natural, but the pin stays and he gets time. } 8. Nd2 Qd6 9. Bh4 { Much better for him: the pin still ties your pieces, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rg8] }) 8. O-O { He castles, and his rook now guards f2. Your queen and bishop point at his king. Which piece joins them? Had his bishop dropped back to e3, your queen would take his b pawn. [%csl Rf2] } 8... Ng4 { Right: the knight joins in, and now f2 and h2 are both in your sights. [%cal Gg4f2,Gg4h2] } (8... Qxb2 { The b pawn is free, but his knight comes out with a hit on your queen. } 9. Nd2 Be6 10. Rb1 { Better for him: your queen won a pawn, but she is far from his king. [%csl Rb2] }) (8... Qe5 { The queen hits his bishop, but his queen defends it. } 9. Qc1 Bd6 10. f4 { Better for him: his pawn chased your queen, and his pieces are safe. [%csl Rf4] }) (8... Be6 { A calm developing move, but his pieces get time to come out. } 9. c3 Qd6 10. Nd2 { Better for him: he chased your queen and is still a pawn up. [%csl Rd6] }) (8... Bg4 { The bishop hits his, but his pawn chases your queen and his bishop takes the knight. } 9. c3 Qe5 10. Bxf6 Qxf6 { Better for him: your knight is gone for his bishop, and you are still a pawn down. [%csl Rf6] }) (8... O-O { Castling is safe, but his pawn chases your queen first. } 9. c3 Qe5 10. Qd2 { Much better for him: your queen was pushed back, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Re5] }) 9. c3 { His c pawn kicks your queen, and that is his mistake: bishop to h4 was the move. Had his bishop taken your knight on g4, the h pawn would take back. Where should your queen go? } 9... Qe5 { Right: your queen hits his bishop on g5 and the pawn on h2, where mate waits. He cannot guard both. Now watch. [%cal Ge5g5,Ge5h2][%tail] } (9... Qd6 { The queen goes back, and hits nothing. } 10. g3 Qg6 11. h4 { Slightly better for him: the pawn on g3 shut your queen out. [%csl Rg3] }) (9... Qxf2+ { The queen takes with check, but his rook takes her. } 10. Rxf2 Nxf2 11. Qb3 Nh3+ 12. Kf1 Nxg5 { Better for him: you have a rook and a bishop for your queen, and his queen hits your pawns on b7 and f7. [%csl Rb3] }) (9... Nxf2 { The knight takes, but your queen is still under attack. } 10. cxd4 Nxd1 11. dxc5 Nxb2 { Much better for him: the queens came off, and you are a bishop down for a pawn. [%csl Rc5] }) (9... Qd7 { The queen goes back, and his pawns chase your pieces. } 10. d4 Bd6 11. h3 { Much better for him: his pawns kicked your bishop, and your knight is next. [%csl Rg4] }) (9... Qd8 { The queen runs home, and his bishop on g5 takes her. } 10. Bxd8 { Lost for you: his bishop took your queen. [%csl Rd8] }) (9... Qd5 { From d5 the queen hits his bishop, but his king pawn takes her. } 10. exd5 { Lost for you: his king pawn swallowed your queen. [%csl Rd5] }) 10. g3 Qxg5 { Your queen takes his bishop: a whole piece for your gambit pawn, and your knight on g4 is guarded twice. Much better for you. [%csl Gg5] } 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the king hunt"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/q8kFt04X"]
[Role "teaching"]
