{ The Stafford position: your bishop points at f2, your knight hits e4, and his king is still in the middle. }
6. h3 { His h pawn takes g4 away from your knight. Time for the king hunt: you give pieces to drag his king into the open, then chase it with checks. Start with bishop takes f2, check. [%cal Yc5f2] } 6... Bxf2+ { Check! His king has to take the bishop: stepping aside loses even more. [%csl Re1] } (6... Nxe4 { Just as good: the other sacrifice, the knight first. Wild too, but we learn the bishop one. } 7. dxe4 Bxf2+ 8. Ke2 Qh4 { Better for him with best play: three pawns for your knight, and his king is stuck on e2. [%csl Re2] }) (6... Be6 { Just as good, and quiet: develop, and he stays a pawn up. } 7. Qe2 Qe7 8. g3 { Better for him: a normal game, and his extra pawn is safe. [%csl Be6] }) (6... h5 { Just as good by the numbers, and your usual pawn move. But his h pawn already guards g4. [%cal Rh3g4] } 7. Nd2 Be6 8. c3 { Better for him: a quiet game, and the knight jump never comes. [%csl Rg4] }) (6... Qd4 { The queen hits f2, but his queen comes out and covers it. } 7. Qf3 Be6 8. c3 { Better for him: his pawn chases your queen next, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (6... O-O { Castling is just as good, and calm. No hunt, no trap. } 7. Be2 a5 8. O-O { Better for him: both kings are safe, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%csl Bg8] }) (6... Qe7 { Just as good: the queen develops, and your king can castle long. } 7. Qe2 Be6 8. g3 { Better for him: a slow game, where his extra pawn is safe. [%csl Be7] }) 7. Kxf2 { His king takes. Now grab his king pawn with the knight, with check. [%cal Yf6e4] } 7... Nxe4+ { Check! Your knight grabs a pawn. If his d pawn takes it, the d column opens and your queen takes his. [%cal Gd8d1] } 8. Kf3 { His king walks forward, his best try; going home to g1 or e1 runs into your queen checks. Chase it with a queen check. [%cal Yd8f6] } 8... Qf6+ { Check! Your knight on e4 still hangs: it is the bait that pulls his king forward. [%csl Ge4] } (8... O-O { Castling first gives him a free move: his queen comes out to guard. } 9. Qe1 Qf6+ 10. Bf4 { Better for him: his queen and bishop guard the king, and your knight on e4 still hangs. [%csl Re4] }) (8... Qd5 { The queen attacks from d5, but his queen comes out with a hit. } 9. Qe2 Qf5+ 10. Bf4 g5 11. Qxe4+ Qxe4+ 12. Kxe4 { Much better for him: the queens came off, and you are two pieces down for a pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (8... Ng5+ { The knight steps back with check, but his king walks away. } 9. Ke2 O-O 10. Qd2 { Much better for him: his king got out, and you are a piece down. [%csl Re2] }) 9. Kxe4 { His king takes your knight, as most players do, and now stands in the middle of the board. Had his bishop blocked on f4, your g pawn would hit it. Castle: your rook comes to the e column. [%cal Ye8g8] } 9... O-O { Castled: your king is safe, and your rook is ready to check down the e column. If his d pawn steps up now, check him from f5. [%cal Gf8e8] } (9... Bf5+ { The bishop check is natural, but his king steps back, and nothing follows. } 10. Ke3 O-O-O 11. Qf3 { Much better for him: his queen covers the king, and you are two pieces down. [%csl Rf3] }) (9... Qh4+ { The queen check drives his king back, and it finds a safe road. } 10. Ke3 Qg3+ 11. Qf3 Qe1+ 12. Be2 Qxh1 13. Nc3 { Much better for him: you won his rook, but you gave two pieces, and your queen is stuck in the corner. [%csl Rh1] }) (9... Kd8 { The king steps aside, but your rook stays shut in. } 10. d4 Re8+ 11. Kd3 { Much better for him: his king walked back to safety. [%csl Rd3] }) 10. Bf4 { His bishop comes out to f4, his only good move: his king back to e3 lets your checks draw. Check him with the rook. [%cal Yf8e8] } 10... Re8+ { Check down the e column! His king has to step back to f3. [%cal Ge8e4] } (10... g5 { Just as good, a little better even: hit the bishop first, and the rook check comes next. } 11. Qd2 Re8+ 12. Kf3 { The same position as the main line, one move later. [%csl Gg5] }) (10... Qf5+ { The queen check lets his king run for cover. } 11. Ke3 Re8+ 12. Kf3 Qh5+ 13. g4 { Much better for him: his king escapes the checks, and you are two pieces down for a pawn. [%csl Rg4] }) 11. Kf3 { His king steps back. His bishop on f4 stands between your queen and his king. Hit it with your g pawn. [%cal Yg7g5] } 11... g5 { The pawn hits the bishop, and the bishop cannot move: your queen pins it to his king. [%cal Gf6f3] } (11... Qxb2 { The b pawn is free, but his knight comes out and your attack stops. } 12. Nd2 Be6 13. Qc1 { Much better for him: his queen offers a trade, and you are two pieces down for two pawns. [%csl Rb2] }) (11... Be6 { Developing looks natural, but it lets his knight come out first. } 12. Nc3 g5 13. Qd2 { Much better for him: his pieces now guard the king. [%csl Rc3] }) 12. Qd2 { His queen guards the bishop. Had he pushed his g pawn instead, the bishop would fall just the same. Take it. [%cal Yg5f4] } 12... gxf4 { That is the king hunt: you won the bishop back, and his king still stands in the open on f3. You are a knight down for a pawn, and with perfect defence he is better; the checks go on, but the lesson stops here. [%csl Rf3] } (12... Be6 { Developing is too slow: his bishop stays, and you are pieces down. } 13. Nc3 Kh8 14. Ne4 { Much better for him: his knight joins the defence with a hit on your queen. [%csl Re4] }) 0-1


