{ The Stafford position again: your bishop looks at f2, and his king side pieces are still at home. }
6. Be3 { His bishop offers a trade, and that is a mistake: bishop to e2 was the move. How do you use it? } 6... Bxe3 { Right: take it. His f pawn has to take back, and his king loses the pawn that guarded it. [%csl Rf2] } (6... Bb6 { Stepping back keeps your bishop, but his bishop stays and fights for your dark squares. } 7. Qd2 Ng4 8. Bxb6 axb6 { Better for him: the bishops came off on his terms, and his pawns are healthy. [%csl Rb6] }) (6... Qd6 { The queen steps up, but his e pawn hits her. } 7. e5 Bb4+ 8. c3 { Much better for him: his pawn chased your queen, and your bishop is hit too. [%csl Rb4] }) (6... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but she just steps up to d2. } 7. Qd2 Qe7 8. Nc3 { Better for him: your bishop on g4 hits nothing, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Rg4] }) (6... Qe7 { The queen guards your bishop, but he trades anyway. } 7. Bxc5 Qxc5 8. Qd2 { Better for him: the bishops came off, and his pawns stayed healthy. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... Be6 { The bishop develops, and leaves your bishop on c5 to his. } 7. Bxc5 { Much better for him: his bishop took yours for free. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... O-O { Castling leaves your bishop on c5 hanging. } 7. Bxc5 { Much better for him: his bishop took yours, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... Bd4 { The bishop steps into the middle, but he trades it off. } 7. Bxd4 Qxd4 8. c3 { Better for him: his pawn chases your queen, and his pawns are healthy. [%csl Rd4] }) (6... Bd6 { The bishop steps back, and the trap is off. } 7. Be2 Be6 8. Nd2 { Better for him: he keeps his bishop and his extra pawn. [%csl Rd6] }) 7. fxe3 { He takes back with the f pawn. Where is he weak now? } 7... Ng4 { Right: the knight hits the pawn on e3 and the pawn on h2. [%cal Gg4e3,Gg4h2] } (7... Qd6 { Just as good: the queen hits e3 and aims at g3. } 8. Be2 h5 9. Nc3 { About level: your queen and pawns point at his king, and he has doubled pawns. [%csl Be3] }) (7... O-O { Safe, but slow: he gets to cover his pawns. } 8. Be2 Be6 9. O-O { Better for him: he castled, and the pawn on e3 is guarded. [%csl Re3] }) (7... h5 { Just as good: your usual pawn move, and the rook may swing out along the sixth row. } 8. Qf3 Rh6 9. h3 { Slightly better for him: his pawn covers g4, and your rook stands out in the open. [%csl Rh6][%cal Rh3g4] }) (7... Be6 { Just as good: the bishop develops, and his doubled pawns will not run away. } 8. Qf3 h5 9. h3 { Slightly better for him: the knight jump is gone, but his pawns stay weak. [%csl Be6] }) (7... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but she steps to d2. } 8. Qd2 Qe7 9. h3 { Slightly better for him: his pawn now kicks your bishop, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rg4] }) (7... Qe7 { Just as good: the queen eyes his weak pawn on e3. } 8. Nc3 Be6 9. Qd2 { Slightly better for him: he guards the pawn, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Be7] }) 8. Qf3 { His queen guards e3 and eyes your f pawn. What now? [%cal Rf3e3] } 8... Qh4+ { Right: check! His best is to block with the g pawn, and your queen stays close to his king. [%cal Gh4e1] } (8... Nxe3 { The knight grabs the pawn, but his queen on f3 guards it. } 9. Qxe3 { Lost for you: his queen took your knight, and one pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Re3] }) (8... O-O { Just as good: castle first, and the check can wait. } 9. h3 Ne5 10. Qh5 { Slightly better for him: your knight went back, but your king is safe. [%csl Be5] }) (8... Qg5 { The queen hits e3 again, but his queen steps up and offers a trade. } 9. Qf4 Qb5 10. b3 { Better for him: your queen wandered to the edge, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rb5] }) (8... Be6 { The bishop develops, and his pawn chases your knight. } 9. h3 Nh6 10. Nc3 { Better for him: your knight went to the edge, and his pawns are guarded. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Qe7 { The queen comes to e7, but his pawn chases your knight. } 9. h3 Ne5 10. Qg3 { Better for him: your knight was pushed back, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Re5] }) (8... Qd6 { The queen aims at g3, but his pawn kicks your knight first. } 9. h3 Qb4+ 10. Nd2 { Better for him: your queen checks, but his knight covers, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rb4] }) 9. g3 { His g pawn blocks the check. Your queen has to move. Where does she do the most? } 9... Qh6 { Right: from h6 she hits e3 again, together with your knight. [%cal Gh6e3,Gg4e3] } (9... Qg5 { Just as good: from g5 the queen hits e3 as well. } 10. Kd2 Qe7 11. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: he is still a pawn up, and your queen is back home. [%csl Be7] }) (9... Nxh2 { The knight grabs h2, but there it is trapped. } 10. Qf2 Qe7 11. Rxh2 { Lost for you: his rook took the knight, and a single pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Rh2] }) (9... Qe7 { The queen goes home, and his pawn on e3 is safe. } 10. Nc3 Ne5 11. Qf4 { Slightly better for him: your pieces were chased back, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Re5] }) (9... Qh5 { The queen stays on the edge, and his knight comes out. } 10. Nc3 Qg5 11. Qf4 { Better for him: he offers a trade of queens, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Rf4] }) (9... Qf6 { The queen offers a trade, and he takes it. } 10. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Better for him: the queens came off, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rf6] }) (9... Qd8 { The queen goes home, and your attack stops. } 10. Nc3 Qg5 11. Qf4 { Better for him: your queen had to come out again, and his pawns are safe. [%csl Rg5] }) 10. Qf4 { His queen offers a trade, his best move. Had his king stepped up instead, your knight would take h2. What now? } 10... Nxe3 { Right: the knight takes e3, and the gambit pawn is back. [%csl Ge3] } (10... g5 { Just as good by the numbers: chase his queen first. } 11. Qf3 Nxh2 12. Qd1 Qd6 13. Rxh2 Qxg3+ 14. Rf2 Qxe3+ { Better for him, as in the main line: you have two pawns for your knight, and his king is open. [%csl Ge3] }) (10... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his queen trades herself off first. } 11. Qxh6 Nxh6 12. h3 { Better for him: your knight went to the edge, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Rh6] }) (10... Qxf4 { Trading queens gives up the pawn on e3: his pawn takes back and guards it. } 11. exf4 h5 12. Ke2 { Better for him: the queens are off, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Re3] }) (10... Qg6 { The queen steps away, and his pawn on e3 is safe for now. } 11. h3 Nf6 12. Nc3 { Better for him: he kept the pawn, and your knight was pushed back. [%csl Rf6] }) (10... O-O { Castling is natural, but the queens come off first. } 11. Qxh6 Nxh6 12. Nc3 { Better for him: your knight went to the edge, and he still has the extra pawn. [%csl Rh6] }) 11. Qxh6 { He trades queens. Take back with the pawn. } 11... gxh6 { The queens are off. Your h pawns are doubled, but the pawn you gave on move three is back. } 12. Kd2 { His king steps up and hits your knight. Where does it go? } 12... Ng4 { Right: the knight goes back to g4, safe and active. The gambit pawn is back and the queens are gone. Honestly, he is still better: your h pawns are doubled. [%csl Gg4] } (12... Nxf1+ { Taking his bishop gives his rook the open f column. } 13. Rxf1 Rg8 14. Nc3 { Better for him: his rook hits your f pawn, and your pawns are weak. [%csl Rf1] }) (12... Nxc2 { The knight grabs another pawn, but it cannot get out. } 13. Kxc2 { Lost for you: his king took the knight, and your pawns are weak as well. [%csl Rc2] }) (12... Nc4+ { A check, but his pawn simply takes the knight. } 13. dxc4 { Lost for you: his pawn took your knight, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rc4] }) 0-1


