{ His knight is out, and your bishop on c5 stares at f2. His king has not castled yet. }
6. Bc4 { His bishop comes out and looks at your king side. It is a natural move, and a mistake: pawn to h3 was his best. How do you make f2 feel it? } 6... Ng4 { Right: knight and bishop both hit f2, and only his king guards it. [%cal Gg4f2,Gc5f2] } (6... Qd4 { The queen hits f2 as well, but his queen comes out and covers. } 7. Qe2 b5 8. Bb3 { Slightly better for him: his queen guards f2, and your queen stands in the open. [%cal Re2f2] }) (6... Bxf2+ { The check wins your bishop back with a fork, but the sharp chance is gone. } 7. Kxf2 Qd4+ 8. Ke1 Qxc4 { About level: the gambit pawn is back, but his king is safe enough and your attack is over. [%csl Gc4] }) (6... O-O { Castling is safe, but his f pawn shuts the door on g4. } 7. f3 b5 8. Bb3 { About level: he keeps his extra pawn, and your knight has lost its square. [%csl Rf3] }) (6... h5 { The pawn gets ready for the h column, but it is too slow here. } 7. Qe2 b5 8. Bb3 { Better for him: his queen guards f2, and you are still a pawn down. [%cal Re2f2] }) (6... Qe7 { The queen develops, but his d pawn guards in time. } 7. d3 Ng4 8. d4 { Better for him: his pawn chases your bishop, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... Be6 { The bishop offers a trade, and your pawns get doubled. } 7. Bxe6 fxe6 8. Qe2 { Better for him: the bishops came off, and your pawns are weak. [%csl Re6] }) (6... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, and his f pawn kicks it straight back. } 7. f3 Be6 8. Bxe6 fxe6 { Better for him: the bishops were traded, and he kept his extra pawn. [%csl Rf3] }) (6... Qd6 { The queen aims at h2, but he guards. } 7. Qe2 { Better for him: his queen covers f2 before your pieces arrive. [%cal Re2f2] }) 7. O-O { He castles, and that is his mistake: guarding f2 with his rook or queen was his best try. His king sits in the corner. What joins your knight and bishop? [%csl Rf2] } 7... Qh4 { Right: queen and knight both hit h2, and mate is threatened. If his bishop grabs your f pawn with check first, step to e7. [%cal Gh4h2,Gg4h2] } (7... Bxf2+ { The bishop takes first, but his rook takes back, and the attack runs dry. } 8. Rxf2 Nxf2 9. Qf1 Qd4 10. Bxf7+ Ke7 11. Qxf2 Qxf2+ 12. Kxf2 { About level: you won a rook, but you gave two pieces and a pawn for it. [%csl Rf2] }) (7... Qd6 { The queen heads for h2 by the long road, and his pawn blocks it. } 8. e5 Qh6 9. h3 { About level: his pawn on e5 cut your queen off, and his h pawn kicks your knight. [%csl Re5] }) (7... O-O { Castling first is too slow: he gets time to guard. } 8. Be2 Qh4 9. Bxg4 Bxg4 10. Qe1 { Level: his bishop swapped off your knight, and the attack is gone. [%csl Rg4] }) (7... Qd4 { The queen hits f2 too, but it is not a check, and his bishop strikes first. } 8. d3 Nxf2 9. Bxf7+ Kxf7 10. Qh5+ Ke6 { Level: your knight took f2, but his bishop and queen dragged your king into the open. [%csl Re6] }) (7... Qf6 { The queen hits f2, but his queen steps up to trade. } 8. Qf3 Qxf3 9. gxf3 { Level: the queens came off, and his pawn now kicks your knight. [%csl Rf3] }) (7... Qe7 { The queen stays back, and his pawns chase your pieces. } 8. d4 Qd6 9. e5 { About level: his pawns chased your pieces, and the attack is gone. [%csl Re5] }) 8. h3 { His h pawn kicks your knight and guards against the mate. Must the knight run? } 8... Nxf2 { Right: the knight takes f2, and the gambit pawn is back. Now it hits his queen and the pawn on h3. [%cal Gf2d1,Gf2h3] } (8... Bxf2+ { Also strong: the bishop takes first. But the knight capture wins more. } 9. Rxf2 Qxf2+ 10. Kh1 h5 { Better for you, but less: you won his rook and a pawn for your bishop. [%csl Gf2] }) (8... Ne5 { The knight retreats, and his d pawn chases your bishop. } 9. d4 Nxc4 10. dxc5 { Better for him: the pieces came off, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rc5] }) (8... h5 { The pawn guards your knight, but his d pawn hits your bishop. } 9. d4 Bb6 10. Na4 { Much better for him: he gained time, and your attack stalls. [%csl Rb6] }) (8... Bd4 { The bishop hits his knight, and from e5 it would aim at h2, but his pawn takes your knight first. } 9. hxg4 Be5 10. g3 { Level: you have some attack for the knight, and nothing more. [%csl Rg3] }) (8... Be6 { The bishop offers a trade, but his bishop takes first. } 9. Bxe6 Nxf2 10. Bxf7+ Kd8 { Slightly better for him: you lost your bishop, but your knight took f2 and his king is open. [%csl Rf7] }) (8... Nh6 { The knight runs to the edge, and his d pawn kicks your bishop. } 9. d4 Be7 10. Qf3 { Much better for him: your attack is over, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rh6] }) (8... Nf6 { The knight goes back, and his pawns chase your bishop. } 9. d4 Bb6 10. a4 { Much better for him: your pieces were pushed back, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rf6] }) 0-1


