{ Both sides are developing: his knight is on c3, and your bishop points at f2. }
6. Be2 { He tucks his bishop on e2, and that is a mistake: pawn to h3 was his best. Can you punish it before he castles? } 6... Qd4 { Right: the queen threatens mate on f2 and hits his king pawn at the same time. [%cal Gd4f2,Gd4e4] } (6... h5 { Your usual pawn move, but here there is something stronger. } 7. d3 Ng4 8. Bxg4 hxg4 { Slightly better for him: your rook got the h column, but he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rd3] }) (6... O-O { Castling is safe, but slow. } 7. O-O a5 8. Kh1 { Better for him: both kings are safe, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Bg8] }) (6... Ng4 { The knight jumps at f2, but his bishop takes it. } 7. Bxg4 Qh4 8. Qf3 Qxg4 9. Qxg4 Bxg4 { Better for him: the queens came off, and his extra pawn is safe. [%csl Rg4] }) (6... Be6 { A calm developing move, and he has time to guard. } 7. d3 Qe7 8. Bf4 { Better for him: he develops, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Be6] }) (6... Qd6 { The queen aims at h2, but he props his pawn up. } 7. d3 { Better for him: his pawn guards e4, and your queen hits nothing. [%cal Rd3e4] }) (6... Qe7 { The queen develops, but his pawns take the middle. } 7. d4 Bb6 8. e5 { Better for him: his pawns chase your pieces, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Re5] }) (6... Bg4 { The bishop offers a trade, but his bishop takes it and your knight cannot take back safely. } 7. Bxg4 Qd4 8. O-O { Much better for him: his bishop took yours, and you have nothing for it. [%csl Rg4] }) 7. O-O { He castles to stop the mate. Pawn to d3 instead would have lost to a queen check on f2. What now? } 7... Nxe4 { Right: the knight takes his king pawn, and the gambit pawn is back. [%csl Ge4] } (7... h5 { The pawn move is too slow now: he props his king pawn up. } 8. d3 Ng4 9. Bxg4 hxg4 { Slightly better for him: your rook has the h column, but he kept his extra pawn. [%csl Rd3] }) (7... O-O { Castling gives him time to guard his king pawn. } 8. d3 Qd8 9. Kh1 { Better for him: your queen went home, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rd8] }) (7... Be6 { Developing is fine, but the pawn was there to take. } 8. d3 O-O-O 9. Be3 { Better for him: his bishop chases your queen, and he keeps the pawn. [%csl Re3] }) (7... Bg4 { The bishop offers a trade, and he simply takes it. } 8. Bxg4 h5 9. Bh3 { Much better for him: his bishop took yours, and you cannot take back safely. [%csl Rh3] }) 8. Nxe4 { His knight takes yours. Take back with the queen. } 8... Qxe4 { Level: the gambit pawn is back, your queen stands in the middle, and your bishop on c5 still aims at his king. [%csl Ge4] } 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: he kicks the knight"]
