{ You are a pawn down, and your knight on f6 guards the middle. }
5. e5 { His pawn kicks your knight. The safe square is d5; the tricky one is e4, as bait. Against his best play the bait costs you about a pawn, but his natural moves fall into traps. [%cal Yf6e4] } 5... Ne4 { Your knight sits in the middle of his camp. If his d pawn kicks it with a single step, leave it there. [%csl Ge4] } (5... Nd5 { The sound retreat, and objectively better. But it sets no traps. } 6. d4 c5 7. c3 { Slightly better for him: a calm game, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Bd5] }) (5... Ng4 { Just as good by the numbers, and the most played. But on g4 the knight sets fewer traps. } 6. d4 Be6 7. Nc3 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and his pawns hold the middle. [%csl Bg4] }) (5... Qe7 { The queen hits his pawn, but she blocks your bishop. } 6. d4 Nd5 7. c4 { Better for him: his pawns chase your knight around. [%csl Re7] }) (5... Nd7 { Just as good by the numbers, even a little better: the knight steps back toward b6. But it sets no traps. } 6. d4 Nb6 7. Nc3 { Better for him: a quiet game, and he keeps his extra pawn. [%csl Bd7] }) (5... Bg4 { Just as good by the numbers: the bishop comes out with a hit on his queen, but it gets traded. } 6. Be2 Bxe2 7. Qxe2 { Better for him: the bishops came off, and his pawn still kicks your knight. [%csl Bg4] }) (5... Ng8 { Objectively better than the bait, but the knight goes all the way home. } 6. Bc4 Qd4 7. Qe2 { Better for him: your knight undid its own move, and his pieces come out. [%csl Rg8] }) 6. d4 { He pushes his d pawn two squares, his best move; the single step walks into a trap. Had his queen hit your knight from e2, it would step back to c5. Bring your queen out to the edge. [%cal Yd8h4] } 6... Qh4 { Your queen hits f2 and guards your knight. A pawn kick at her from g3 would be a mistake: remember that later. [%cal Gh4f2] } (6... Be6 { Just as good: the bishop develops, and the game stays calm. } 7. c3 f5 8. Bd3 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and nothing is hanging. [%csl Be6] }) (6... f5 { Just as good: the pawn props up your knight. } 7. Bc4 b5 8. Be2 Be6 { Better for him: your knight is safe, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Bf5] }) (6... Be7 { Just as good: develop the bishop first, and the queen can come later. } 7. Bc4 b5 8. Be2 { Better for him: your pawn chased his bishop back, but he keeps the pawn. [%csl Be7] }) (6... c5 { The pawn hits his centre, but your knight on e4 has no guard, and his queen hits it. } 7. Qe2 Bf5 8. g4 { Much better for him: his pawn chases the bishop that guards your knight. [%csl Re4] }) (6... Bf5 { The bishop guards the knight, but his bishop comes to e3 and his pawn to g4 next. } 7. Be3 f6 8. Bd3 { Much better for him: your pieces are tangled, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rf5] }) 7. Qf3 { His queen guards f2 and hits your knight, as most players choose; your queen still guards it. Develop your dark bishop: e7. [%cal Yf8e7,Rf3f2] } 7... Be7 { Your bishop is out, and your king can castle. Against his best play he is a pawn up and better. Now a pawn kick from g3 would be safe for him: his queen on f3 guards that pawn. [%csl Ge7] } (7... Ng5 { Just as good: the knight steps back and hits his queen. } 8. Qg3 Qxg3 9. hxg3 { Better for him, with the queens off: he is a pawn up. [%csl Bg5] }) (7... f5 { Just as good: the pawn guards your knight. } 8. Qf4 Be7 9. Qxh4 Bxh4 { Better for him: the queens came off, and his extra pawn stays. [%csl Bf5] }) (7... Bb4+ { Just as good: the check first, and the bishop drops back to e7 after. } 8. c3 Be7 9. Bd3 { Better for him: the same position, with his pawn on c3. [%csl Bb4] }) (7... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but she simply takes your knight. } 8. Qxe4 { Lost for you: a knight down. [%csl Re4] }) 0-1


