{ Your bishop stares at f2, and your knight hits his king pawn. His king is still at home. }
6. Bg5 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen, and that is his mistake: bishop to e2 was the move. The pin looks strong. Is it? } 6... Nxe4 { Right: the knight grabs e4 and walks out of the pin with a threat. Now your queen hits his bishop on g5. [%cal Gd8g5] } (6... Bxf2+ { Also good: the check first wins pawns. But the knight move sets a bigger trap. } 7. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 8. Ke1 Nxg5 { Much better for you: you won two pawns and kept your pieces, but his queen is safe. [%csl Gg5] }) (6... Qd4 { The queen steps out of the pin and hits f2, but his bishop drops back. } 7. Bh4 Qxb2 8. Nd2 { Level: you took a pawn on b2, but the trap is gone. [%csl Bb2] }) (6... h6 { Asking the bishop is natural, and he simply steps back. } 7. Be3 Bxe3 8. fxe3 { Slightly better for him: the bishops came off, and the chance has passed. [%csl Be3] }) (6... Be7 { Breaking the pin with the bishop is safe, but it gives up f2. } 7. Nc3 O-O 8. Be2 { Better for him: he is a pawn up and develops in peace. [%csl Re7] }) (6... Qd6 { The queen steps off the pin, and the trap is gone. } 7. Bh4 g5 8. Bg3 { Better for him: the pin is broken, but you are a pawn down. [%csl Rg3] }) (6... O-O { Castling is natural, but the pin stays and the trap is gone. } 7. Nd2 h6 8. Bh4 { Better for him: he keeps the pin, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rh4] }) 7. Bxd8 { He takes your queen, as three players in four do. That is the losing choice: queen to e2 was his best try. Now what? } 7... Bxf2+ { Right: check! A pawn falls, and his king has only one square: e2. [%cal Gf2e1] } (7... Kxd8 { Taking back the bishop throws it all away: his pawn takes your knight with check. } 8. dxe4+ Bd6 9. Nd2 { Lost for you: he took your queen and your knight, and you got only his bishop. [%csl Rd8] }) (7... Nxf2 { The wrong piece takes: without a check, he has time. } 8. Qh5 { Lost for you: his queen escapes with a hit on your bishop and your f pawn, and you are a queen down. [%csl Rh5] }) 8. Ke2 { Forced: e2 was the only square. Can you finish it? } 8... Bg4# { Checkmate! Your two bishops and your knight box his king in: taking your queen cost him the game. Had his pawn taken your knight instead, the check on f2 would still have won his queen. [%csl Re2] } (8... Kxd8 { Taking back the bishop throws away a mate in one: his pawn takes your knight with check. } 9. dxe4+ Ke7 { Lost for you: your queen and knight are gone for his bishop, and bishop to g4 was mate. [%csl Rg4] }) (8... Nc3+ { A knight check, but his knight takes it, and the mate is gone. } 9. Nxc3 Bd4 { Lost for you: his knight took yours, and you are a queen down; bishop to g4 was mate. [%csl Rc3] }) 0-1


