{ Your knight on g4 is bait, and your queen aims at his king's corner. }
9. hxg4 { He takes the bait, as one player in four does, and that is his mistake: pawn to e5, blocking your queen, was the only move. How do you punish it? } 9... hxg4 { Right: your pawn takes back, and the h column is open. Your queen and rook both aim at his king. If his bishop takes on g4, your queen mates on h2. [%cal Gh8h2,Gd6h2] } (9... Bxg4 { The bishop takes back, but the h column stays shut. } 10. Nd2 Qg6 11. Nc4 { Much better for him: you are a knight down, and your rook never reached his king. [%csl Rh8] }) (9... Qh2+ { The queen checks, but nothing guards her there. } 10. Kxh2 { Lost for you: his king took your queen. [%csl Rh2] }) 10. g3 { He blocks the diagonal with his g pawn, as most players do. His toughest try was pawn to e5, which only delays the mate. Can you finish now? } 10... Qxg3# { Checkmate! His f pawn cannot take your queen: your bishop on c5 pins it to his king. [%cal Gc5g1] } (10... Qg6 { Too slow: his king gets time to run. } 11. Kg2 Be6 12. Rh1 { Much better for him: his rook took the h column, and you are a knight down. [%csl Rh1] }) 0-1


