{ The bait on g4 is still there, with your queen behind it on the road to h2. }
9. Bxg4 { His bishop takes the knight, the most common choice, and that is a mistake: pawn to e5 was his only good move. What now? } 9... hxg4 { Right: the pawn takes back, and now it hits his pawn on h3. If his bishop comes to e3, take it. [%cal Gg4h3] } (9... Bxg4 { The bishop takes back, and the attack on his king is gone. } 10. Qd2 Bxh3 11. d4 { Slightly better for him: you won a pawn back, but your attack has run out. [%csl Rd4] }) (9... Qh2+ { The queen check fails: his king simply takes her. } 10. Kxh2 { Lost for you: the king took her, and your attack is over. [%csl Rh2] }) 10. e5 { His pawn hits your queen, as most players choose. His best was pawn to d4. Where does your queen go? } 10... Qg6 { Right: from g6 your queen stays near his king and guards your pawn on g4. Now watch. [%tail] } (10... Qe7 { The queen steps back, and his d pawn gains time. } 11. d4 Bb6 12. c3 { Better for you, but much less: your queen left his king alone. [%csl Be7] }) (10... Qd4 { The queen goes to the middle, but his c pawn chases her. } 11. c3 Qd8 12. d4 { About level: the attack stalled while your queen ran around. [%csl Bd8] }) (10... Qh6 { His bishop takes her: the h6 square is guarded. } 11. Bxh6 { Lost for you: your queen is gone for nothing. [%csl Rh6] }) (10... Qd8 { Also good, but slower: the queen goes all the way home. } 11. Nc3 { Better for you, but less: your queen left his king alone, and his knight comes out. [%csl Rc3] }) (10... Qd5 { The queen goes to the middle, but his knight chases her. } 11. Nc3 Qd8 12. Ne4 { Level: your queen was chased back, and the attack stalled. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Qe6 { The queen offers a trade on g4, and he takes the pawn with it. } 11. Qxg4 Qxg4 12. hxg4 { Slightly better for him: the queens came off, and your attack with them. [%csl Rg4] }) 11. d4 gxh3 { The gambit pawn is back, your pawn on h3 sits next to his king, and your queen and rook aim at it: much better for you. [%csl Gh3] } 0-1


