{ Your bishop gave itself up, and your knight has just grabbed a pawn with check. You are a piece down: your task is to hold the draw. }
8. Kg1 { His king runs home to g1, as most players choose. You are a piece down, and his king is safe from mate. Can you save the game? } 8... Qd4+ { Right: check! His king has only one square, h2. [%cal Gd4g1] } (8... O-O { Castling is too slow: his queen comes out, and the checks are gone. } 9. Qe1 Ng5 10. Qf2 { Much better for him: you let his king off the checks, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rg1] }) (8... Ng3 { The knight hits his rook, but it is not check, and his queen strikes first. } 9. Qe1+ Be6 10. Qxg3 { Lost for you: his queen took your knight, and you are two pieces down. [%csl Rg3] }) (8... Be6 { The bishop develops, but the checks were the point. } 9. Qe1 Qd4+ 10. Qe3 { Much better for him: his queen came out, and the checks are over. [%csl Re3] }) 9. Kh2 { His king steps to h2, its only square. What now? } 9... Qe5+ { Right: check again, and his king must go back to g1. [%cal Ge5h2] } (9... Qd6+ { Just as good: the check from d6 sends his king back too. } 10. Kg1 Qd4+ { Level: the same checks, and the same draw. [%csl Gd4] }) (9... Nf2 { The knight forks his queen and rook, but it is not check: his queen escapes with a threat. } 10. Qf3 Nxh1 11. Kxh1 { Much better for him: the material is level, but your king is stuck in the middle and his pieces come out fast. [%csl Rh1] }) (9... O-O { Castling lets his king breathe. } 10. Qf3 Ng5 11. Qe3 { Much better for him: the checks are over, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rh2] }) (9... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his king gets a free move. } 10. Qf3 { Much better for him: his queen joins the defence, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rf3] }) 10. Kg1 { Back to g1, the only square. Keep going. } 10... Qd4+ { Right: check once more. His king can only shuttle between g1 and h2. Now watch. [%tail] } (10... Ng3 { The knight hits his rook, but it is not check, and his queen comes out. } 11. Qf3 O-O 12. Qf4 { Much better for him: the checks stopped, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rg3] }) 11. Kh2 Qe5+ 12. Kg1 Qd4+ { The same position for the third time: a draw by repetition. A piece down, you saved the game, because his king could never escape the checks. [%csl Gd4] } 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: answer the king on e1"]
