{ Your knight on e4 gives check. His king must choose a square. }
8. Ke1 { His king runs back to e1, as one player in four does, and that is his mistake: king to f3 was the move. Find the finish. } 8... Qh4+ { Right: check! His king cannot return to f2: your knight on e4 covers it. Now watch. [%cal Ge4f2,Gh4e1][%tail] } (8... Ng3 { The knight hits his rook, but it is not check, and his king escapes. } 9. Kf2 Ne4+ 10. Kf3 { Better for him: his king slipped back to f3, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rf3] }) (8... Be6 { Too slow: his queen comes out and guards. } 9. Qf3 Qh4+ 10. g3 { Better for him: his queen and pawn cover the king, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rf3] }) (8... Qd4 { The queen hits f2, but it is not a check, and his pawn takes your knight. } 9. dxe4 Qxe4+ 10. Qe2 { Lost for you: you gave two pieces for two pawns, and the attack is over. [%csl Re2] }) (8... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but she takes it. } 9. Qxg4 Nc5 10. Qxg7 { Lost for you: his queen took your bishop and then your g pawn. [%csl Rg7] }) 9. Ke2 Qf2# { Checkmate! Your knight guards your queen on f2, and his king has no square left. Pawn to g3 would only have delayed the same mate. [%csl Gf2] } 0-1


