{ Your knight has taken on f2, and it hits his queen and his pawn on h3. }
9. Qf3 { His queen escapes, as one player in three plays, and with his bishop it hits f7. His best was to take that pawn with the bishop, with check. Who strikes first? } 9... Nxh3+ { Right: check! The knight jumps to h3, and your queen stares down the h column at his king. Now watch. [%cal Gh4h1][%tail] } (9... Nxe4+ { A check, but his pawn blocks it, and the attack fades. } 10. d4 Bxd4+ 11. Kh2 { Slightly better for you, but his king escaped the worst. [%csl Rh2] }) (9... Be6 { You guard f7, but the chance for mate is gone. [%cal Ge6f7] } 10. Rxf2 Bxf2+ 11. Qxf2 Qxf2+ 12. Kxf2 Bxc4 { Better for you, but less: the queens came off, and you are the exchange up. [%csl Gc4] }) (9... Nd3+ { A discovered check, but it gives his king a safe square. } 10. Kh1 Nf2+ 11. Rxf2 Qxf2 { Slightly better for you: you won the exchange, but the mate is gone. [%csl Gf2] }) (9... Ng4+ { Also a discovered check, but it is the wrong square. } 10. Kh1 Nf2+ 11. Rxf2 Qxf2 { Slightly better for you: the knight came back to f2 and was taken; you won the exchange, not the game. [%csl Gf2] }) 10. Kh1 Nf2+ { Discovered check: the knight steps away, and your queen checks down the h column. [%cal Gh4h1] } 11. Kg1 Qh1# { Checkmate! Your knight guards your queen, and your bishop guards your knight. King to h2 would have lasted longer, but his queen would have fallen. [%cal Gf2h1,Gc5f2] } 0-1


