{ Your knight has jumped to e4, into the middle of his camp. }
6. d3 { His d pawn kicks your knight, as one player in five does, and that is his mistake: pawn to d4 was the move. Does the knight have to move? } 6... Bc5 { Right: the knight stays as bait, and your bishop comes to c5, staring at f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } (6... Nc5 { The safe retreat, and he simply stands better. } 7. d4 Na4 8. Be2 { Better for him: your knight walked back twice, and he took the middle. [%csl Ra4] }) (6... Nxf2 { A knight for a pawn, grabbed too early. } 7. Kxf2 Bc5+ 8. Ke1 Qh4+ 9. g3 { Much better for him: his king escaped, and you gave a knight for one pawn. [%csl Re1] }) (6... Ng5 { The knight retreats, and his pawn takes the middle. } 7. d4 Bf5 8. Bc4 { Better for him: he gained time, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rg5] }) (6... Bb4+ { A check first, but his pawn blocks, and your knight is still attacked. } 7. c3 Bc5 8. d4 { Much better for him: his pawns chased your bishop back, and the trap is gone. [%csl Rd4] }) (6... Qh4 { The queen guards the knight and hits f2, but his pawn takes the knight anyway. } 7. dxe4 Qxe4+ 8. Qe2 { Much better for him: his queen blocks, and you are a knight down. [%csl Re2] }) (6... Qd4 { The queen guards the knight, but his pawn takes it all the same. } 7. dxe4 Qxe4+ 8. Qe2 { Much better for him: you won a pawn back, but the knight is gone. [%csl Re4] }) (6... Bf5 { The bishop guards the knight, but his pawn takes it and the queens come off. } 7. dxe4 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 Bxe4 { Much better for him: you took a pawn back, but you are a knight down. }) 7. dxe4 { He grabs the knight, as two players in three do. That is the losing choice: bishop to e3 was his best try. Now what? } 7... Bxf2+ { Right: check! The bishop pulls his king away, and his queen on d1 stays opposite yours. [%cal Gd8d1] } (7... Qxd1+ { Trading queens first leaves you a piece down. } 8. Kxd1 Bxf2 9. Nd2 { Much better for him: you have two pawns for your knight. [%csl Rd1] }) (7... Qh4 { Your queen hits f2, but without a check he has time. } 8. Qf3 Bd4 9. Nc3 { Much better for him: his queen guards f2, and you are a knight down. [%cal Rf3f2] }) (7... Qd4 { The queen hits f2 and his pawn, but he trades queens. } 8. Qxd4 Bxd4 9. f4 { Lost for you: the queens came off, and you are a knight down. [%csl Rd4] }) (7... Qe7 { The queen steps up, but without a check he has time. } 8. Bd3 Be6 9. Qe2 { Much better for him: his pieces cover the king, and you are a knight down. [%csl Re2] }) 8. Ke2 { His king steps up to e2, as most players do; taking the bishop would lose his queen at once. Queen takes queen looks tempting. Is there more? } 8... Bg4+ { Right: check! Your bishop hits his king, and his queen stands right behind it on the same diagonal. [%cal Gg4d1] } (8... Qxd1+ { Trading queens throws the win away. } 9. Kxd1 Be6 10. Ke2 { Much better for him: the queens are off, and you are a knight down. [%csl Rd1] }) (8... Qh4 { The queen joins the attack, but his knight comes out and blocks. } 9. Nd2 Bg4+ 10. Nf3 { Slightly better for you, but the queen was there to win. [%csl Gf3] }) (8... Bd4 { The bishop saves itself, and his queen is safe. } 9. h3 Be6 10. c3 { Much better for him: you are a knight down, and the trap is over. [%csl Rc3] }) 9. Kxf2 { His king takes your bishop. Collect. } 9... Qxd1 { Your queen takes his queen. You gave a knight and a bishop for his queen and a pawn. Now watch. [%tail] } (9... Bxd1 { Also winning: the bishop takes the queen. But your queen should do the taking. } 10. Nc3 Bxc2 { Much better for you, but your queen stayed at home. [%csl Gc2] }) (9... Qh4+ { A check instead of taking the queen: his pawn blocks and hits yours. } 10. g3 f5 11. gxh4 { Lost for you: his pawn took your queen. [%csl Rh4] }) 10. Nc3 Qxc2+ { Your queen escapes with a check and takes another pawn. A queen for a knight and a bishop, and nothing of yours hangs: much better for you. [%csl Gc2] } 0-1


