 Much better for him: you won one pawn back, but the knight is gone. [%csl Re2] }) (8... O-O { Castling gives him time to take your knight. } 9. dxe4 Qh4+ 10. Kd2 { Much better for him: his king walked out, and you are a knight down. [%csl Rd2] }) (8... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his pawn takes your knight. } 9. dxe4 Qh4+ 10. Kd2 { Much better for him: your check came too late, and the knight is gone. [%csl Re4] }) 9. g3 { His g pawn blocks the check. Is that the end of it? } 9... Nxg3 { Right: the knight takes g3 and hits his rook in the corner. [%cal Gg3h1] } (9... Qg5 { The queen retreats, and his pawn takes your knight. } 10. dxe4 Qxe3+ 11. Be2 { Much better for him: his pawn took your knight, and your queen won only a pawn back. [%csl Re4] }) (9... Qe7 { The queen steps back, and your knight is lost. } 10. dxe4 { Much better for him: your knight is gone for nothing. [%csl Re4] }) (9... Qh6 { The queen steps back, and his pawn takes your knight. } 10. dxe4 Qxe3+ 11. Be2 { Much better for him: you took a pawn with check, but you are a knight down. [%csl Re2] }) (9... Qf6 { The queen hides on f6, but his pawn takes her. } 10. exf6 { Lost for you: your queen is gone, taken by a pawn. [%csl Rf6] }) 10. hxg3 { His h pawn takes your knight, and the h column opens. What did that leave behind? } 10... Qxh1 { Your queen takes the rook in the corner. You gave the knight and got his rook and a pawn: much better for you. [%csl Gh1] } (10... Qxg3+ { A check and a pawn, but the rook was worth more, and his king runs. } 11. Kd2 g6 12. Nc3 { Much better for him: one pawn does not pay for your knight, and his king walked away. [%csl Rd2] }) (10... Qe7 { The queen runs home, and the rook in the corner stays his. } 11. Nc3 Qxe5 12. Qf3 { Lost for you: you gave the knight for one pawn. [%csl Rh1] }) 0-1


