wn blocks and your bishop hangs. } 10. c3 Qxh1 11. cxb4 { Better for him: you won the rook, but gave the knight and the bishop. [%csl Rb4] }) (9... Qxd4 { The queen grabs a pawn, but leaves his rook alone. } 10. Nd2 Bg4 11. Nb3 { Much better for him: you took two pawns for the knight, but his rook is safe. [%csl Rb3] }) 10. Nc3 { His knight comes out. Your queen in the corner has few squares, and his bishop wants g2. What now? } 10... Bh3 { Right: your bishop covers g2 and hits his bishop, which your queen pins to his king. You gave the knight and won his rook: much better for you. [%cal Gh3f1][%csl Gg2] } (10... Bb4 { The bishop pins his knight, but it does nothing about g2. } 11. Be3 Qd5 12. Bg2 { Better for you, but less: your queen left the corner, and his bishop reached g2. [%csl Rg2] }) (10... Be6 { The bishop develops, but g2 is left for his bishop. } 11. Bd2 h5 12. O-O-O { Better for you, but less: your queen is boxed in the corner. [%csl Rh1] }) (10... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but it does not cover g2. [%csl Rg2] } 11. Bh6 gxh6 12. O-O-O { Slightly better for you: the rook is yours, but his king escaped and his pieces are active. [%csl Gh1] }) (10... Be7 { The bishop comes out to e7, but the g2 square stays open for his bishop. } 11. Bf4 Bb4 12. O-O-O { About level: your queen is boxed in the corner, and his pieces are out. [%csl Rh1] }) 0-1
